WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.160
 - Hello and welcome to Chapter 13, Web Services.

00:00:03.160 --> 00:00:05.840
 So what we've been doing so far is we've been using

00:00:05.840 --> 00:00:08.420
 the request-response cycle, we've learned about sockets,

00:00:08.420 --> 00:00:11.020
 we've learned about URL lib, and we've actually learned

00:00:11.020 --> 00:00:14.920
 how to pull HTML and even flat text off the internet.

00:00:14.920 --> 00:00:17.440
 But what we're going to talk about now is using that same

00:00:17.440 --> 00:00:20.520
 request-response cycle to retrieve information

00:00:20.520 --> 00:00:24.580
 that is specifically designed for programmatic consumption

00:00:24.580 --> 00:00:27.320
 so that we had to have this beautiful soup

00:00:27.320 --> 00:00:31.980
 which sort of did a hack job or solve the hard problem

00:00:31.980 --> 00:00:35.700
 of parsing HTML, but why not produce data in a format

00:00:35.700 --> 00:00:38.160
 that makes good sense to a program because programs

00:00:38.160 --> 00:00:39.500
 want to talk to each other.

00:00:39.500 --> 00:00:42.260
 If you recall, the whole idea of a socket is to have

00:00:42.260 --> 00:00:45.060
 one application process sending data

00:00:45.060 --> 00:00:47.360
 to another application process.

00:00:47.360 --> 00:00:51.060
 And so if we think about this for a moment and we realize

00:00:51.060 --> 00:00:53.920
 that we have all these programs, they could be written

00:00:53.920 --> 00:00:56.640
 in different programming languages and they're all connected.

00:00:56.640 --> 00:00:59.040
 And so they might want to send data back and forth

00:00:59.040 --> 00:01:03.120
 through the network, PHP programs, JavaScript programs,

00:01:03.120 --> 00:01:07.860
 Java programs, and so we have to decide on a protocol

00:01:07.860 --> 00:01:10.000
 that is independent of any programming language,

00:01:10.000 --> 00:01:12.680
 and then we call that the wire protocol because if you were

00:01:12.680 --> 00:01:15.800
 to sort of take some connection and watch the exact

00:01:15.800 --> 00:01:18.560
 characters that go back and forth, that's what you would see

00:01:18.560 --> 00:01:20.560
 if you were monitoring the wire.

00:01:20.560 --> 00:01:23.220
 So that's why we call that the wire protocol.

00:01:23.220 --> 00:01:28.120
 And so the idea is, is that we have to agree on a format

00:01:28.120 --> 00:01:30.160
 that is going to represent the data and we can't make it

00:01:30.160 --> 00:01:33.300
 a Python-specific format or a Java format.

00:01:33.300 --> 00:01:37.260
 And when we take the data from the internal representation,

00:01:37.260 --> 00:01:39.780
 maybe a Python dictionary, to send it to the wire,

00:01:39.780 --> 00:01:42.320
 we call that act serialization and that is going from

00:01:42.320 --> 00:01:44.920
 sort of the internal representation to the serial

00:01:44.920 --> 00:01:47.280
 representation or the wire representation.

00:01:47.280 --> 00:01:50.640
 And then here is an example of a person with a name

00:01:50.640 --> 00:01:53.440
 and phone number with using less than and greater than,

00:01:53.440 --> 00:01:56.200
 this is an XML example, and then in the far end

00:01:56.200 --> 00:01:58.200
 in a different programming language, it receives this

00:01:58.200 --> 00:02:01.240
 and then deserializes it and then turns it into some

00:02:01.240 --> 00:02:04.320
 useful structure inside that programming language.

00:02:04.320 --> 00:02:07.560
 And so this is an example of a wire protocol

00:02:07.560 --> 00:02:09.880
 that's using XML and that's one of the formats

00:02:09.880 --> 00:02:11.580
 we're going to talk about.

00:02:11.580 --> 00:02:14.600
 Another format that we're going to talk about is a format

00:02:14.600 --> 00:02:18.920
 called JSON, JavaScript Object Notation, and it is simpler

00:02:18.920 --> 00:02:23.260
 and easier, but it's not as precise and descriptive

00:02:23.260 --> 00:02:24.100
 as XML is.

00:02:24.100 --> 00:02:26.280
 And so while you'll find that most of the things

00:02:26.280 --> 00:02:28.480
 you run into, especially if you're talking to APIs

00:02:28.480 --> 00:02:31.400
 of one form or another, you'll find that JSON

00:02:31.400 --> 00:02:34.400
 is very common, XML still holds sway

00:02:34.400 --> 00:02:35.600
 in places like documents.

00:02:35.600 --> 00:02:38.960
 So if you look at docx at the end of a Microsoft Word

00:02:38.960 --> 00:02:42.560
 document, docx means that it's an XML version

00:02:42.560 --> 00:02:46.240
 of the representation of a word processing document.

00:02:46.240 --> 00:02:48.620
 So the first thing we'll talk about is XML.

